HSH(1)                         Simple Shell                        HSH(1)

NAME
       hsh - minimal UNIX command interpreter

SYNOPSIS
       ./hsh
       echo "command" | ./hsh

DESCRIPTION
       hsh is a basic UNIX shell implemented in C as part of the
       Holberton School program.

       It prints a prompt, reads user input, interprets commands,
       resolves executable paths, and runs programs using
       fork() and execve().

       The shell supports both interactive and non-interactive usage.

FEATURES
       - Displays a prompt in interactive mode
       - Reads user input with getline
       - Parses commands separated by spaces
       - Resolves commands via the PATH environment variable
       - Executes programs using fork and execve
       - Handles SIGINT (Ctrl+C)
       - Supports built-in commands: exit and env

BUILT-IN COMMANDS
       exit [status]
              Terminates the shell.
              Without arguments, it exits using the last command status.
              With a status argument, exits with status modulo 256.
              If the argument is invalid, exits with status 2.

       env
              Displays the current environment variables.

PATH RESOLUTION
       - Commands starting with '/' or './' are treated as direct paths
       - Otherwise, the shell searches in directories listed in PATH
       - If not found, an error message is displayed

ERROR HANDLING
       - Command not found: returns exit status 127
       - Permission denied or invalid execution: returns status 126
       - Invalid exit argument: returns status 2

EXIT STATUS
       The shell returns the status of the last executed command.

EXAMPLES
       Interactive mode:
              $ ./hsh
              $ ls
              $ exit

       Non-interactive mode:
              echo "/bin/ls" | ./hsh

